    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention silent\" data-user-id=\"969501\">Nathan Howell</span> <a href=\"#narrow/channel/583339-AI-authored-projects/topic/Selberg.20Majorant.20improvement.20With.20AI/near/600106865\">said</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><span class=\"user-mention silent\" data-user-id=\"1070783\">Arij Aziz</span> <a href=\"#narrow/channel/583339-AI-authored-projects/topic/Selberg.20Majorant.20improvement.20With.20AI/near/600092902\">said</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>if you have any more advice, i will be happy to hear.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>try using a prompt like this with a frontier model (Opus/Codex) in extra-high thinking:</p>\n<p>thank you for this detailed suggestion. I ran it on codex and other models as well.<br>\nno issues was reported.<br>\nthe responses i got were.</p>\n<p><strong>Summary for the Author:</strong> This repository features an exceptionally clean and well-structured Lean 4 formalization. Your use of <code>AssumptionsRegistry.lean</code>, <code>Audit.lean</code>, and structural hypotheses for future work (rather than polluting the context with <code>sorry</code> or <code>axiom</code>) is a masterclass in proof hygiene.</p>\n<p>Mathematically, the current <code>Core/</code> provides a solid foundational API for finite discrete sums and the basic combinatorial setup of the Selberg sieve. However, the instantiated results (Theorems 1 &amp; 2) act as toy models (single-prime evaluation and universal Cauchy-Schwarz bounds) rather than substantive analytic sieve theorems.</p>\n<p>Author-sendable summary: the repo is sorry-free and builds, and it formalizes a clean finite one-divisor majorant example plus useful algebraic infrastructure. To support the strongest sieve-majorant claims, the next step is a genuine multi-prime/divisor system with optimized Selberg weights, remainder/asymptotic estimates, and at least one nontrivial instantiated arithmetic application.</p>\n<h2>Summary for the Author</h2>\n<p>The repo is technically solid — zero sorry, correct axiom footprint, honest README, good structure — and the Kinetic Propagation theorem (Theorem 3) is the most substantial piece: a Lipschitz propagation bound for multiplicative functions under prime-level perturbation, with the H-functional stability as a bonus, which does not appear verbatim in standard references and is proved in full generality. The quadratic form stability (Theorem 4) is also useful infrastructure.</p>\n<p>The concrete results (Theorems 1 and 2) are correct but the \"improvement\" is a computation on an explicit two-valued function on <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>⋅</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Fin(d⋅m)</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.1389em;\">F</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">in</span><span class=\"mopen\">(</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">d</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">⋅</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">m</span><span class=\"mclose\">)</span></span></span></span> — this is a <strong>single-prime sanity check</strong>, not a domain theorem. The restriction lower bound is elementary Cauchy–Schwarz.</p>\n<p>as far as AI verification goes, I have ticked every checklist.</p>\n</blockquote>",
        "id": 600130868,
        "sender_full_name": "Arij Aziz",
        "timestamp": 1780590888
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention silent\" data-user-id=\"1070783\">Arij Aziz</span> <a href=\"#narrow/channel/583339-AI-authored-projects/topic/Selberg.20Majorant.20improvement.20With.20AI/near/600130868\">said</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>However, the instantiated results (Theorems 1 &amp; 2) act as toy models (single-prime evaluation and universal Cauchy-Schwarz bounds) rather than substantive analytic sieve theorems.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>I'm not familiar with this space but that seems like something the model is recommending as a next step - does it work for non-toy models?</p>",
        "id": 600131521,
        "sender_full_name": "Nathan Howell",
        "timestamp": 1780591066
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>The purpose of this project is to get an aknowledgment from Zulip that I have at the very least submitted something that can actually be read and not be slop.<br>\nThe real improvements/advancement, such generalization to multi-prime, will be after I know that I have gotten the format of presentation right.<br>\nThat is why the current results and claims are very tame.</p>",
        "id": 600131647,
        "sender_full_name": "Arij Aziz",
        "timestamp": 1780591099
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention silent\" data-user-id=\"969501\">Nathan Howell</span> <a href=\"#narrow/channel/583339-AI-authored-projects/topic/Selberg.20Majorant.20improvement.20With.20AI/near/600131521\">said</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><span class=\"user-mention silent\" data-user-id=\"1070783\">Arij Aziz</span> <a href=\"#narrow/channel/583339-AI-authored-projects/topic/Selberg.20Majorant.20improvement.20With.20AI/near/600130868\">said</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>However, the instantiated results (Theorems 1 &amp; 2) act as toy models (single-prime evaluation and universal Cauchy-Schwarz bounds) rather than substantive analytic sieve theorems.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>I'm not familiar with this space but that seems like something the model is recommending as a next step - does it work for non-toy models?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Yes. The AI was presenting next steps. Which was my original motivation anyway. But I want to move forward after it is confirmed by the zulip community that this user (me) at least knows how to submit a verifiable result.</p>",
